      "content": "Of course! When you're making the /s/ sound, imagine a group of goblins trying to pass by a sleeping dragon without waking it up. They move silently, barely making a sound, which is like your vocal cords staying relaxed and not vibrating. The air flows smoothly through your mouth, creating a hissing sound. This is the unvoiced /s/ sound.\n\nNow, when you switch to the /z/ sound, think of those same goblins trying to wake the dragon up with a loud, clear shout. This is like your vocal cords coming together and vibrating as you push air through your mouth. The buzzing sound you hear is the result of this vibration. So, in the larynx, the vocal cords are relaxed and not vibrating for the /s/ sound, but they are vibrating for the /z/ sound. This is the physical mechanism behind the difference between these two sounds. Just remember, when you're practicing, think of the goblins' whispers and shouts!",
